Just to gently clarify the terminology before we dive in: you are looking for **Inter-Thread Communication** (communication *between* two different threads). "Intra-thread" would mean communication inside a single thread, which doesn't require any special methods since it just executes line-by-line!
To completely understand this, we are going to build a simple **Data Courier System**.
 * We have a **Receiver Thread** that needs a data packet to do its job.
 * We have a **Sender Thread** that creates the data packet.
Here is the exact purpose of why we write these methods, followed by the code that proves it.
### 1. The Purpose of wait()
**Why we write it:** We write wait() to explicitly tell the JVM, *"My thread has reached a point where it literally cannot continue because it is missing data. Do not let my thread burn CPU cycles spinning in a loop. Put it to sleep, take away its lock key, and DO NOT wake it up until someone else provides the data."*
### 2. The Purpose of notify() / notifyAll()
**Why we write it:** We write notify() to act as an alarm clock for other threads. We are telling the JVM, *"I just updated the shared data! If there are any threads currently asleep in the waiting room because they were waiting for this data, wake them up right now so they can come get it."*
### The Code Example
Here is a perfectly thread-safe implementation. I have heavily commented it so you can see exactly *why* each line is written.
```java
public class InterThreadCommunicationDemo {

    // This is the shared data package
    private String dataPacket = null;
    
    // We create a dedicated lock object just for communication
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    // THE RECEIVER (Uses wait)
    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    public void receiveData() {
        synchronized (lock) {
            System.out.println("Receiver: Entered the room. Checking for data...");

            // THE 'WHILE' LOOP: We must always check the condition in a loop!
            // If the data is null, we cannot proceed.
            while (dataPacket == null) {
                System.out.println("Receiver: No data found. Calling wait() and going to sleep... 💤");
                try {
                    // PURPOSE OF WAIT: Go to sleep and drop the key so the Sender can enter!
                    lock.wait(); 
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                }
                // When the thread wakes up, it loops back up to check if dataPacket is still null.
            }

            // If we break out of the while loop, it means dataPacket is NOT null!
            System.out.println("Receiver: ⏰ I woke up! The data is here: [" + dataPacket + "]");
            System.out.println("Receiver: Processing complete.");
            
            // Reset the packet so the cycle can start over if needed
            dataPacket = null; 
        }
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    // THE SENDER (Uses notify)
    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    public void sendData(String message) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            System.out.println("Sender: Entered the room. Writing the data...");
            
            // Simulating heavy work to create the data
            try { Thread.sleep(2000); } catch (InterruptedException e) {}
            
            dataPacket = message;
            System.out.println("Sender: Data written! Calling notifyAll() to wake up the Receiver... 📢");

            // PURPOSE OF NOTIFY: Tell the sleeping Receiver that the condition has changed!
            lock.notifyAll(); 
            
            System.out.println("Sender: Notify sent, but I am still holding the lock until my method finishes.");
        } // The lock is officially dropped right here.
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    // MAIN METHOD (Firing the Threads)
    // ---------------------------------------------------------
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        InterThreadCommunicationDemo system = new InterThreadCommunicationDemo();

        // Thread 1: The Receiver
        Thread receiverThread = new Thread(() -> system.receiveData());

        // Thread 2: The Sender
        Thread senderThread = new Thread(() -> system.sendData("Secret Launch Code"));

        // We start the receiver first so it gets stuck waiting
        receiverThread.start();

        // Give the receiver a tiny head start to ensure it goes to sleep
        try { Thread.sleep(100); } catch (InterruptedException e) {}

        // Now we start the sender
        senderThread.start();
    }
}

```
### The Output Breakdown (What happens in the RAM)
If you run this code, here is exactly what the console prints and what is happening internally:
**1. Receiver: Entered the room. Checking for data...**
The Receiver thread grabs the lock. It checks the while(dataPacket == null) loop. Because the packet is null, it moves inside the loop.
**2. Receiver: No data found. Calling wait() and going to sleep... 💤**
The Receiver hits lock.wait(). The JVM physically rips the thread off the CPU, puts it in the WAITING state, and **forces it to drop the lock key on the floor**.
**3. Sender: Entered the room. Writing the data...**
Because the Receiver dropped the key, the Sender thread is now able to grab the lock and enter its synchronized block! (If wait() didn't drop the lock, the Sender would be permanently blocked and you'd have a deadlock).
*(2-second pause while Sender simulates work...)*
**4. Sender: Data written! Calling notifyAll() to wake up the Receiver... 📢**
The Sender populates the dataPacket string and hits notifyAll().
*Note: The Receiver wakes up inside the JVM, but it CANNOT run yet. It is in the BLOCKED state because the Sender is still holding the lock key!*
**5. Sender: Notify sent, but I am still holding the lock until my method finishes.**
The Sender finishes its printing, hits the closing } brace, and finally drops the key.
**6. Receiver: ⏰ I woke up! The data is here: [Secret Launch Code]**
The Receiver grabs the key the Sender just dropped. It finishes the wait() method, checks the while loop again, sees that dataPacket is no longer null, breaks out of the loop, and prints the success message!
